1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,663
Every server runs software that someone had to install. Doing it by hand does not scale.

2
00:00:06,213 --> 00:00:06,935
On Ubuntu,

3
00:00:06,935 --> 00:00:09,389
a package manager does it for you.

4
00:00:09,389 --> 00:00:11,049
A package is a program,

5
00:00:11,049 --> 00:00:13,287
plus the list of what it needs.

6
00:00:13,287 --> 00:00:15,813
Apt finds packages in repositories,

7
00:00:15,813 --> 00:00:17,690
and D P K G installs them.

8
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:20,443
Today you read the repository list,

9
00:00:20,443 --> 00:00:21,513
inspect packages,

10
00:00:21,513 --> 00:00:22,708
preview an install,

11
00:00:22,708 --> 00:00:24,533
and learn the update routine.

12
00:00:26,583 --> 00:00:29,682
Where do packages come from?

13
00:00:29,682 --> 00:00:31,674
From repositories,

14
00:00:31,674 --> 00:00:33,776
listed in one file.

15
00:00:33,776 --> 00:00:35,658
Type: grep ^URIs,

16
00:00:35,658 --> 00:00:37,207
then the path.

17
00:00:37,757 --> 00:00:38,722
Two addresses.

18
00:00:38,722 --> 00:00:40,377
The main Ubuntu archive,

19
00:00:40,377 --> 00:00:42,583
and a separate security archive.

20
00:00:42,583 --> 00:00:44,306
Each one is a repository.

21
00:00:45,256 --> 00:00:49,160
Now the suites. Type: grep ^Suites, and the same path.

22
00:00:50,710 --> 00:00:52,722
Suites are release channels.

23
00:00:52,722 --> 00:00:56,172
Noble is Ubuntu twenty-four oh four as released.

24
00:00:56,172 --> 00:00:58,543
Noble dash updates carries fixes.

25
00:00:58,543 --> 00:01:01,633
Noble dash security carries security fixes.

26
00:01:02,683 --> 00:01:08,571
Now ask about one package. Type: apt-cache policy curl | head -n 4.

27
00:01:09,121 --> 00:01:11,297
Installed is the version you have.

28
00:01:11,297 --> 00:01:14,817
Candidate is the newest version the repositories offer.

29
00:01:14,817 --> 00:01:15,777
If they differ,

30
00:01:15,777 --> 00:01:17,121
an update is waiting.

31
00:01:19,171 --> 00:01:26,531
What is installed? Ask the package database. Type: dpkg -s curl | head -n 4.

32
00:01:27,081 --> 00:01:32,926
Status says install ok installed. The database is the record of what is on this machine.

33
00:01:33,876 --> 00:01:40,916
Which package owns a file? Type: dpkg -S /usr/bin/curl.

34
00:01:41,466 --> 00:01:46,160
The curl package owns it. Use this when you find a file and wonder where it came from.

35
00:01:47,110 --> 00:01:54,256
And which files does a package put on disk? Type: dpkg -L curl | grep bin.

36
00:01:54,806 --> 00:01:57,345
The bin folder, and the curl program itself.

37
00:01:59,395 --> 00:02:01,322
Always preview first.

38
00:02:01,322 --> 00:02:03,341
Dash s means simulate,

39
00:02:03,341 --> 00:02:05,085
so nothing changes.

40
00:02:05,085 --> 00:02:09,123
Type: apt-get -s install cowsay | tail -n 6.

41
00:02:09,673 --> 00:02:12,316
Two packages would be installed: cowsay,

42
00:02:12,316 --> 00:02:14,166
and a library it depends on.

43
00:02:14,166 --> 00:02:16,478
Apt works out dependencies for you.

44
00:02:17,428 --> 00:02:20,820
The Inst lines are the plan, and the dependency comes first.

45
00:02:21,770 --> 00:02:27,914
Now preview a removal. Type: apt-get -s remove curl | tail -n 4.

46
00:02:28,464 --> 00:02:32,603
It would remove curl. Read what will be removed before you agree to anything.

47
00:02:33,553 --> 00:02:35,568
How many updates are waiting?

48
00:02:35,568 --> 00:02:37,792
Type: apt-get -s upgrade | grep,

49
00:02:37,792 --> 00:02:40,849
and keep the line that starts with a number.

50
00:02:41,399 --> 00:02:45,730
The first number is how many packages could be upgraded. Your number will differ.

51
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:50,197
Now the commands that change the system.

52
00:02:50,197 --> 00:02:52,371
They need sue do and a real machine,

53
00:02:52,371 --> 00:02:54,607
so these screens are an illustration.

54
00:02:55,557 --> 00:02:57,075
First: sudo apt update.

55
00:02:57,075 --> 00:03:00,573
It refreshes the package lists from the repositories.

56
00:03:00,573 --> 00:03:01,893
It installs nothing.

57
00:03:02,843 --> 00:03:08,389
Then: sudo apt install, and the package name. Apt shows the plan, and asks you to confirm.

58
00:03:08,939 --> 00:03:13,462
To bring everything current: sudo apt upgrade. Update first, then upgrade.

59
00:03:14,012 --> 00:03:18,727
Remove takes the program away. Purge also deletes its configuration files.

60
00:03:19,677 --> 00:03:24,733
Then: sudo apt autoremove. It removes dependencies that nothing needs any more.

61
00:03:25,283 --> 00:03:30,232
Add repositories only from sources you trust. Read the plan, then answer yes.

62
00:03:32,282 --> 00:03:33,605
Four myths to clear up.

63
00:03:34,155 --> 00:03:40,171
Myth one: apt update installs updates. No. Update refreshes the list. Upgrade installs.

64
00:03:40,721 --> 00:03:46,139
Myth two: remove deletes everything. No. Purge also deletes the configuration.

65
00:03:46,689 --> 00:03:52,044
Myth three: any download is safe if it installs. No. Use trusted repositories.

66
00:03:52,594 --> 00:03:56,647
Myth four: answer yes to every prompt. No. Read the plan first.

67
00:03:57,197 --> 00:03:59,288
Update, read the plan, then upgrade.

68
00:04:01,338 --> 00:04:03,663
Here is how you check your own understanding.

69
00:04:04,213 --> 00:04:07,648
Read the repository list, and say where packages come from.

70
00:04:08,198 --> 00:04:10,673
Compare the installed and candidate versions.

71
00:04:11,223 --> 00:04:14,444
Find which package owns a file, with D P K G.

72
00:04:14,994 --> 00:04:18,130
Preview an install with a simulation, and read the plan.

73
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:22,179
Explain update, upgrade, remove, purge and autoremove.

74
00:04:22,729 --> 00:04:24,499
Say how to add software safely.

75
00:04:25,049 --> 00:04:26,671
To pass, you need all six.

76
00:04:28,721 --> 00:04:29,392
Your turn,

77
00:04:29,392 --> 00:04:31,003
in your virtual machine.

78
00:04:31,003 --> 00:04:32,480
These tasks only read,

79
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:33,285
or simulate.

80
00:04:33,285 --> 00:04:34,694
Nothing is installed.

81
00:04:35,244 --> 00:04:38,188
One: read your repository list, and name the suites.

82
00:04:38,738 --> 00:04:43,538
Two: run apt-cache policy for a package, and compare installed with candidate.

83
00:04:44,088 --> 00:04:49,038
Three: use D P K G dash s and dash capital S on a program you use.

84
00:04:49,588 --> 00:04:54,494
Four: simulate an install of a small package, with dash s, and read the plan.

85
00:04:55,044 --> 00:04:56,923
Five: run sudo apt update,

86
00:04:56,923 --> 00:04:59,452
then apt list dash dash upgradable.

87
00:04:59,452 --> 00:05:01,764
Update only refreshes the lists.

88
00:05:02,314 --> 00:05:05,514
Six: simulate a removal, and say what purge would add.

89
00:05:06,064 --> 00:05:09,371
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

90
00:05:24,921 --> 00:05:25,796
Quick check.

91
00:05:26,346 --> 00:05:28,607
Which command refreshes the package lists?

92
00:05:29,157 --> 00:05:31,013
Which flag simulates an install?

93
00:05:31,563 --> 00:05:34,848
Which D P K G option finds the owner of a file?

94
00:05:35,398 --> 00:05:37,724
Does remove delete configuration files?

95
00:05:38,274 --> 00:05:44,887
Objective covered: MC04-O07.

96
00:05:45,437 --> 00:05:47,506
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

97
00:05:58,056 --> 00:06:00,936
Next lesson: disks, filesystems and mounts.

98
00:06:01,486 --> 00:06:04,814
